MESSAGE {"head": "# Implement and prove a HumanEval task correct in K \u2014 using the provided reference semantics\\n\\nYou are working entirely inside the current directory. Everything you produce\\nmust be written here. The K toolchain (`kompile`, `krun`, `kprove`) ", "length": 2903, "line": 9, "phase": null, "role": "user", "sha256": "41949fc8bd3a4aa8f285cbfd040dc70807695225dcfe5c1a88dfa3b030a5d43d", "tail": "rogress available.\\n\\nThis ordered precedence is total and mutually exclusive. Incomplete\\ndeliverables or a later Gate status do not create a second marker. This runner\\nmarker reports positive proof execution only, not a `VALIDATED` judgment."}
MESSAGE {"head": "I\u2019ll inspect the task prompt, translator constraints, and supplied K modules first. Then I\u2019ll implement the narrowest translatable solution, build concrete and symbolic definitions, and iterate on `krun`/`kprove` until the positive proof co", "length": 296, "line": 13, "phase": "commentary", "role": "assistant", "sha256": "4d95eae0bd550cc2c1371e8ae31e4e1f77423a25fde27ecb47a1d238e9a3f3b8", "tail": "d supplied K modules first. Then I\u2019ll implement the narrowest translatable solution, build concrete and symbolic definitions, and iterate on `krun`/`kprove` until the positive proof commands close or a genuine semantics blocker is isolated."}
MESSAGE {"head": "The task is `count_up_to(n)`: return all primes strictly below a non-negative `n`. The semantics supports integer arithmetic, mutable lists, loops, and calls, but an unbounded symbolic loop will need an explicit invariant/abstraction in `ve", "length": 381, "line": 26, "phase": "commentary", "role": "assistant", "sha256": "ec24ed8f3e89e70210802fa34f297379ab97822e00dd3b254b475ab223ccb738", "tail": "loops, and calls, but an unbounded symbolic loop will need an explicit invariant/abstraction in `verification.k`; I\u2019m reading the control-state shapes now so the proof remains tied to actual execution rather than only checking the examples."}
MESSAGE {"head": "The implementation now passes all prompt examples in CPython and translates cleanly. I\u2019ve chosen a simple exhaustive-divisor algorithm because its correctness condition is transparent: each candidate survives exactly when no integer from `2", "length": 445, "line": 68, "phase": "commentary", "role": "assistant", "sha256": "0d79fe33651a5a3883933cd221e6c2f29ce750828830e2ce73f621c80c12ddcf", "tail": "ves exactly when no integer from `2` through `candidate - 1` divides it; the K proof abstraction is tied to the complete translated loop body, so changing the algorithm invalidates that proof match. The symbolic definition is compiling now."}
MESSAGE {"head": "The universal symbolic entry-body claim now closes with `#Top` for arbitrary `N >= 0`. I\u2019m moving to the independent concrete leg next: LLVM compilation of the untouched reference semantics and execution of translated assertions for every e", "length": 417, "line": 97, "phase": "commentary", "role": "assistant", "sha256": "0c599625964f802c53cde982ee4296d98441365407db8173c7881ad1bd3a4984", "tail": "ce semantics and execution of translated assertions for every example in the prompt. After that I\u2019ll add a mutation probe so the proof script demonstrates that its implementation-specific invariant does not silently certify an altered loop."}
MESSAGE {"head": "The concrete LLVM run completed with `<k> .K`, `NoExc`, and exit code `0`; all six prompt assertions were exercised under the supplied semantics. I\u2019m strengthening the symbolic argument before finalizing: instead of leaving the whole outer ", "length": 444, "line": 137, "phase": "commentary", "role": "assistant", "sha256": "7644f0aca3e5c1d6c0e635d50cdcc921335619a33244e29d257c7344a900ba2f", "tail": " instead of leaving the whole outer loop as one trusted summary, I\u2019m decomposing it into separately proved inner-loop, outer-loop, and entry-body claims, with recursive K functions defining \u201cno divisor\u201d and the exact ordered prime sequence."}
MESSAGE {"head": "The strengthened inner-loop claim is not closing promptly; it is consuming CPU in symbolic exploration rather than finding the intended circularity. I\u2019m stopping that run before it pressures the 8 GB limit, then I\u2019ll inspect a bounded proof", "length": 347, "line": 189, "phase": "commentary", "role": "assistant", "sha256": "ee708bc4f85223e061cf0246685ad2a026a72a70c8fce2df796ad9b0800f5129", "tail": "er than finding the intended circularity. I\u2019m stopping that run before it pressures the 8 GB limit, then I\u2019ll inspect a bounded proof trace/residual state and adjust the invariant shape instead of falling back to the earlier coarse summary."}
MESSAGE {"head": "The narrowed invariant shape fixed the issue: the inner divisor-loop theorem now independently proves `#Top` in about two seconds against the base semantics, with no loop-summary rule available. I\u2019m using that proved theorem as the sole add", "length": 277, "line": 293, "phase": "commentary", "role": "assistant", "sha256": "f84dbdb257c8d3ab4e5392cc21f660bd31b2d91d3274e0fd38f64e69ce2c367c", "tail": "e issue: the inner divisor-loop theorem now independently proves `#Top` in about two seconds against the base semantics, with no loop-summary rule available. I\u2019m using that proved theorem as the sole added rule for the outer-loop proof now."}
MESSAGE {"head": "The outer-loop induction also closes with `#Top`. Its post-state is the recursive `primesAcc(VS, I, N)` definition, which appends a candidate iff the independently proved divisor scan returns true; this gives increasing order and excludes c", "length": 431, "line": 352, "phase": "commentary", "role": "assistant", "sha256": "6af1e432045847c8536ae9da456b7f36ca8c2714ea40120b21bcdb0e08eee1f7", "tail": " true; this gives increasing order and excludes composites by construction. I\u2019m now checking the entry theorem and the `n = 0/1` boundary theorem, then I\u2019ll make the reproducible script use fresh build directories so it can be rerun safely."}
MESSAGE {"head": "All four symbolic claims have now produced `#Top`: inner-loop induction, outer-loop induction, the universal `N >= 2` entry theorem, and the `0 <= N < 2` boundary theorem. The script also hashes the regenerated `solution.mpy` before proving", "length": 401, "line": 386, "phase": "commentary", "role": "assistant", "sha256": "9df4e2440d1dcb693cdd97b2dc9ff1e008f9c078d09d925314a64f8b824281e6", "tail": "y theorem. The script also hashes the regenerated `solution.mpy` before proving, so the claims cannot be reused unchanged after a source mutation. I\u2019m doing one clean end-to-end run of `prove.sh` now to validate the exact recorded commands."}
MESSAGE {"head": "Implemented and verified:\\n\\n- [solution.py](/work/solution.py) and generated [solution.mpy](/work/solution.mpy)\\n- [verification.k](/work/verification.k) with mathematical prime-sequence definitions and layered loop lemmas\\n- [spec.k](/work/sp", "length": 564, "line": 415, "phase": "final_answer", "role": "assistant", "sha256": "b525d0f4535bf1fc0a91150bf29f8f57fea2fbe6aa366e0aa941f4302aeb3607", "tail": "execution and Haskell proofs\\n\\n`./prove.sh` exited 0. All prompt examples passed under `krun`, and all four `kprove` commands printed `#Top`.\\n\\nRESULT: KPROVE_PASSED \u2014 All required positive proof commands printed #Top and exited successfully."}
